WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
Superman: You're letting him kill

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:02.360
Martha.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.240
Martha.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.840
Batman: Why did you say that name?

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.560
Lois: It's his mother's name.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.240
Jim Kakalios What are the odds of two men at random,

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.920
both having mothers name, Martha?

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.280
Hi, I'm Jim Kakalios.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.840
I'm a Physics Professor at
the University of Minnesota,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.400
and the author of The
Physics of Superheroes.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.400
Today, we're going to look at the physics

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.400
behind some DC Universe films.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.760
Must not have been too deep.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.880
Wow, that would be, like, about 11 meters,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.880
you know, 30 to 40 feet up.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.600
I did a little bit of research.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.520
It turns out that there's a whole sport

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.760
that is high diving into shallow pools.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.200
[laughing] And the world record is, like,

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.400
11.5 meters

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.040
that someone jumped and fell into a pool

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.280
that had only 30 centimeters,
about a foot of water.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.240
If you just drop an object from,

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.720
say, 11, 12 meters,

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.200
and ask how fast is it going
when it reaches the bottom,

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.480
if you would neglect air resistance,

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.120
it's going about 44 meters per second,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.120
maybe 80, 90 miles per hour.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.000
But if you come in horizontal,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.840
you're maximizing air resistance.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.000
And so you're converting your

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.760
gravitational potential
energy into kinetic energy,

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.960
but some of that energy goes into work,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.840
pushing the air out of the way,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.640
because you have a large surface area.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.320
And so the amount of
energy available to you

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.440
as kinetic energy is reduced.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.960
So your velocity is reduced.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.960
A parachute travels much faster

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.080
when it's tied up in the backpack

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.360
than when it's spread out.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.000
When the parachute is spread out,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.680
it falls much slower than in the backpack.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.320
So, similarly, the Joker comes down

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.720
the way these extreme
jumpers do, horizontally,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.280
to maximize his air drag

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.040
and decrease his speed.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.560
So instead of 44 meters per second,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.080
let's say he's coming in
at 20 meters per second.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.840
Let's say it's about 45 miles per hour.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.920
The force is still quite high.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.680
It's about over 3,000 pounds to stop him

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.960
in the space of about 1 meter,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.560
once he hits the water.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.640
But there, again, if he
could stretch out the time

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.520
by spreading out that force over

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.440
as much of a large area as possible,

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.440
the pressure is reduced.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.720
In fact, the world record for highest jump

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.440
into shallowest water,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.600
11.52 meters height

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.000
into 30 centimeters of water,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.640
was by Darren Taylor.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.200
Joker may have actually beaten that,

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.360
but I give the Joker an eight out of 10,

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.000
'cause I wanna stay on Mr. J's good side.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.520
[crashing]

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.800
Let's ignore issues like air drag

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.080
[laughing] and his source of energy,

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.680
that he would need to eat constantly

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.040
in order to have enough
caloric energy to run so fast.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.000
In the human brain,
processes happen faster

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.000
than we tend to experience them here.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.520
But no signal gets sent
from neuron to neuron

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.440
at a speed that's roughly
faster than a millisecond,

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.560
a thousandth of a second.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.120
The blink of an eye is 100 milliseconds.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.720
So that's still very fast,
compared to the processes

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.920
on the timescales that we live.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.520
Let's assume that the Flash
is running at a stride

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:58.680
that he covers a meter,
3.3 feet, in a millisecond.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.280
So that's 1,000 meters per second.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.800
1,000 meters per second
is 2,250 miles per hour,

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.040
which turns out to be almost exactly

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.200
three times the speed of sound.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.840
So he could travel at Mach 3.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.600
He can't actually run
on a vertical surface,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.480
because in order to run,
we require friction.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.480
You push backwards with your foot,

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.960
and the ground, because
forces come in pairs,

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.320
the ground pushes back on
you and propels you forward.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.320
But that frictional force
requires there to be

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:34.320
some weight on the surface
that you're pressing down.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.720
If the wall is completely vertical,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.080
then no part of his weight
is pressing against the wall.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.960
Maybe his first step
presses against the wall,

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.480
but then it no longer does.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:50.640
If he's traveling at 2,250 miles per hour,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.160
you can ask how fast does he fall

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.800
due to gravity in that timescale,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.960
and in about a millisecond
he's only fallen

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.480
about 10 to the -6 meters,

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.400
which is about the width
of a red blood cell.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.400
So he could appear to be
running across the wall

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.320
and not fall down because
the effect of gravity,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.680
like, the amount that he
falls is imperceptible.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.600
But, again, if he's trying to go faster

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:23.200
than the human mind can respond,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:26.240
then this is why, if he
doesn't look where he's going,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.120
he's gonna trip.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.000
We speedsters have to stick together,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.760
and he gets an eight out of 10.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:32.520
Superman: Martha.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.080
Batman: Why did you say that name?

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.840
Lois: It's his mother's name!

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.680
Jim: What are the odds
of two men at random

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.320
both having mothers named Martha?

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.760
This movie that came out
just a few years ago,

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:48.120
and each of them is in their 30s.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:53.080
Then their parents were
born in the '50s or '60s.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.480
How common was the name Martha back then?

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.040
In the United States, at least.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.840
Not that common. [laughs]

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.480
Martha in the 1950s was only

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.360
the 49th most popular female name.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:10.400
In the 1960s it was the 94th
most popular female name.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.280
Now, of course, these characters' names

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:13.640
were set in the comic books

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.880
that go back to the '40s and the '50s.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.440
In the 1930s, Martha was
the 24th most popular name

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.120
in America for women.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.960
So the question is, how do we
calculate this probability?

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:27.120
If the case is given

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.360
Bruce has a mother named Martha,

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.560
what is the probability that Clark

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.440
has a mother named Martha?

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.120
Well, then the probability
Bruce has a mother

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.880
named Martha is 1, because
we're specifying it.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.200
So it's just multiplied by the probability

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.680
that Clark has a mother named Martha.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.960
So that way, if it's in the 1960s,

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.880
the odds of this happening are only 0.2%.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:55.480
If you just say Bruce and Clark
are two random individuals,

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.200
what is the probability that they each

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.080
have a mother named Martha?

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.080
The probability goes way down.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.680
Think about a dice.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.040
You have six sides to a dice.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.640
What's the probability
that you roll a one?

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.520
One out of six, 16%.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.240
What's the probability
that you roll two ones?

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.400
Snake eyes, with two die.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.720
Well, you need one-sixth on one

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.600
times one-sixth on the other.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.080
It's one outcome out of
36 possible outcomes,

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.440
or 2.7%.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.040
So it's much less likely if you're saying

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:29.560
two random individuals,
what's the probability

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:31.960
that their mothers are both named Martha?

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:34.760
If they were born in the 1930s,

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:38.640
it's 0.005%.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.600
If it's in the 1960s,

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:44.960
it's 0.0004%.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:48.280
Let's assume that they're
using the names in this movie

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:49.600
that were set in the comics

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.200
that were coming back in the 1930s.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.760
Then they'd get a little bit
of a boost in probability,

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.120
but not much. Still very unlikely.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.880
I would have to give this

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.280
no better than a two out of 10.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.840
[orchestral music]

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.600
Wonder Woman, I'm not gonna complain

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.000
about issues of her strength
or speed and reflexes.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.440
We're gonna grant the character a one-time

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:20.800
miracle exemption from the laws of nature.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.360
In the comics, it was said
that Wonder Woman's bracelet

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.760
was made of Amazonian metal,

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:30.720
which is why she was able
to deflect the bullets.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.600
We can actually figure out

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:35.880
how strong the bracelet would have to be.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.360
We look at the momentum
of a bullet coming in.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.400
Bullet has a weight of maybe 20 grams,

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:45.640
but it's going 1,000 feet per second,

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.840
approximately, let's say.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:51.720
Deflects back, so it has a
momentum in and a momentum out.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.200
So there's a change in momentum

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.480
of twice the initial momentum.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:58.120
To change the momentum requires a force

00:08:58.120 --> 00:08:59.800
applied for a given time.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.840
If we say the time of the
ricochet is a millisecond,

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:04.480
a thousandth of a second,

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:07.560
then the force the bracelet has to supply

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:10.480
is about 2,700 pounds.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:13.760
Quite high. The surface area is very low.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.880
So the pressure that the
bullet exerts on the bracelet:

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.720
70,000 pounds per square inch.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:22.840
What kind of metal can handle that?

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:24.360
Pretty much all of them.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.520
Cold rolled steel, stainless steel.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.400
They're all strong enough to
support a compressive pressure

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:34.280
of 70,000, 75,000 pounds per square inch.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:36.760
I'm not gonna argue with her strength,

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:40.440
but the bracelet itself, I
give this a 10 out of 10.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.440
Lucius: It's called memory
cloth. Notice anything?

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:45.680
Jim: If you're gonna dress up like Dracula

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.840
to beat up criminals,
you gotta have a cape.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:49.440
Lucius: Regularly flexible.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:51.880
But put a current through it...

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.320
molecules realign; becomes rigid.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:59.560
Jim: Shape-memory fabrics.
Then becomes rigid;

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:02.200
you can use it for hang gliding.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.400
Those things actually exist.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.520
We're used to phase transitions,

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:09.960
where, say, ice melts; becomes water.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.280
Or water boils; goes from the liquid

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:14.760
to the vapor phase, become steam.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.120
But there are other types
of phase transitions

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.200
that we've discovered

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.200
where materials go from
one crystal structure

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:22.880
to a different crystal structure.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.600
And that's pretty much a
one-way transformation.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:28.560
Some materials can have a
reversible transformation.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:33.280
You can undo it by warming
or some other process.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:36.720
It used to be that the
shape-memory materials were metals

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.600
for the most part, like nitinol,

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.680
which is used in eyeglass frames.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.640
Presumably, Wayne Technology
has developed a fabric

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:46.800
that, via the application
of an electric field

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.360
of maybe some electric
current passing through it,

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:53.280
undergoes this phase transition
and undergoes this change.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:55.960
There is the Wearable Technology Lab

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.480
at the University of Minnesota,

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.920
where they're developing
materials that compress

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:03.280
under the application,
say, of a temperature,

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.400
that are being used to develop
new suits for astronauts.

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:10.360
Since it's Batman, I
give it eight out of 10.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:14.720
[screaming]

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:16.880
I was gonna say, that's
gonna leave a mark.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:22.720
Jon Osterman is accidentally
locked into a chamber

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.800
that removes his intrinsic field.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.800
In the comics, it is explained
that the intrinsic fields

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.840
are all the fundamental
forces except gravity.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.920
Well, the fundamental forces
of nature, there are four,

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.840
gravity, and then
there's electromagnetism,

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:41.320
the strong nuclear force, which
holds the nucleus together,

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:42.680
and the weak nuclear force

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:45.680
is responsible for some
radioactive decays.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:47.360
Without electromagnetism,

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.600
there's nothing to hold
the atoms together.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:50.800
Without the strong force,

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:53.480
there's nothing to hold
the nuclei together.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.760
And so he would be actually taken apart

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:58.960
at the very subatomic level.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.040
Once he is reborn as Doctor Manhattan,

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:06.400
he seems to have independent control

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:08.840
of his quantum mechanical wave function.

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:10.400
So, quantum mechanics says that

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:12.520
every object can be described

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:15.480
by a mathematical function
called the wave function

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.560
that contains all the
information about the object.

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:22.680
If he is able to access
his total wave function,

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:25.040
it has the entire history

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:26.560
of the object that's contained in it,

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:28.520
which is why he experiences time

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:30.840
as backwards and forwards as well.

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:32.920
If you can control over
your wave function,

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:35.760
there's a phenomenon called
quantum mechanical tunneling

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.880
that would enable you
to go from one position

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.160
to another instantaneously.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.960
So presumably that's what he's
doing when he's teleporting.

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:46.760
And from the "wave" in
the term "wave function,"

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:50.280
there is a wave aspect to nature,

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:54.280
which enables certain phenomena
like diffraction patterns.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:57.480
You send light through a grating,

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.720
and you see a series of spots

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:02.640
due to the fact that the
light has interference

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:04.960
through the multiple slits.

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:08.240
You send an electron through a grating,

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.360
and it shows the same type
of interference pattern,

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.120
even though it's a particle.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:16.080
And that's because the
wave function interacts

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.560
with the slits the same way light does.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:22.280
Doctor Manhattan presumably
is diffracting himself.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:25.960
And that's why we see multiple
versions of him in the clip.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:28.000
So I'll give him an eight out of 10.

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:31.520
[rousing orchestral music]

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:40.440
Superman changing the flow of time.

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.320
He does this in the comics all the time,

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:43.880
like, traveling faster,

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:47.680
so fast that he breaks the "time barrier."

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.080
Well, how fast is he going here?

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:51.400
We can figure that out

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:53.560
by looking at the distance he travels

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:55.240
and how long it takes him.

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:57.640
And the ratio of those two is his speed.

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:00.560
We know the radius of the Earth.

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:03.680
It's a bit over 6,300 kilometers,

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:06.400
so we can conclude that
the radius of his orbit

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.640
is a bit over 10,000 kilometers.

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:12.000
All right, so the distance he goes

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.840
in one orbit is 2 pi R.

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:19.120
So it's 60,000 kilometers in one orbit.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:20.920
How long does that take?

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:23.880
Well, we can count how long it takes him

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.880
to make, say, 10 orbits.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:29.560
And we can conclude that his speed

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:34.360
is about 400 to 500
million meters per second.

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:38.720
The speed of light is 300
million meters per second.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:42.560
So he's going much faster
than the speed of light.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:47.560
So he gets points for
indeed violating physics

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:50.200
[laughing] and doing that.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.280
Of course, he loses points,

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:53.800
because if you're going that fast,

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.760
I don't know how you're
constantly changing direction.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:58.360
Because in order to change direction,

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:01.720
you need some other force
to be able to pull you in

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.720
towards the center of your circular orbit.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:06.280
So he loses points for that.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:08.920
So I would say, overall, five out of 10.

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:20.840
Aquaman talks to fishes.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:23.440
If you give him a miracle exemption

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:26.040
from the laws of nature
to account for the power,

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:29.440
this part is just perfectly fine.

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:33.200
When you think, there are
electrical currents in your brain.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:35.560
And they're constantly changing direction,

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:39.520
stopping, starting, moving
from one neuron to another.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:41.360
Any change in electrical current

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:43.400
creates an electromagnetic wave.

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:45.160
It's the basis of radio.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.480
So you create electromagnetic waves

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:49.960
just by you thinking like that.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:52.760
These are extremely weak
electromagnetic waves,

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:55.880
roughly a billion times
weaker than the radio waves

00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.000
that are in the room with you right now.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:15:59.320
We never think about the fact that

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:01.080
we're surrounded by radio waves,

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:03.280
unless we can't get a cellphone signal.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.760
Aquaman has presumably

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:10.760
a special ability to be sensitive

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:14.200
to these very weak electromagnetic waves

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:15.920
when he thinks

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:19.800
and when he's communicating
with other species.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:23.240
Seawater is a million
times more conductive

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:28.000
to electric fields than
regular deionized water.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.120
So, he's in seawater,

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:32.080
which is a great conductive medium

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:33.600
for the electromagnetic waves.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.920
The fishes have a special organ

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.080
to detect these electromagnetic waves.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:39.800
Sharks in particular,

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:41.560
their organs are so sensitive

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:45.320
that you take a AA battery, 1 1/2 volts,

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:49.840
and you attach one wire
up in, say, Boston,

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:51.640
off in the Boston Harbor,

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:54.400
and the other wire off
the coast of Florida,

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:58.280
and the shark would be able
to detect that electric field.

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:00.080
Extremely sensitive.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:01.640
Once you make the miracle exemption

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.640
that he has this ability,

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:06.840
I give Aquaman 10 out of 10.

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:09.840
[suspenseful music]

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:15.760
Superman's X-ray vision.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:20.320
We can see when light
shines into our eyes,

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:24.720
either directly from a light
or reflected off of an object.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:28.400
Kryptonians apparently can also see

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.320
by emitting light,

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:34.200
being both the source of
illumination and the detector.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:36.000
He can apparently emit light

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:39.200
in both the high-energy part
of the spectrum, X-rays,

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:41.000
and the low-energy part of the spectrum,

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.040
infrared light, heat vision.

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:44.720
X-rays come,

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:47.680
the wavelength of an X-ray
is about the size of an atom,

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:50.440
and it gets scattered by
the electrons in an atom.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:53.560
The more electrons, the
more the scattering.

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:56.920
Water has 10 electrons, H2O molecule.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:17:59.720
The calcium in your
bones has 20 electrons.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.040
So the bones, your skeleton,

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:04.920
are much more effective
at scattering X-rays

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.120
than the best of your tissues,

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:08.720
which is why your skeleton shows up

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:11.920
so starkly in an X-ray image.

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.120
They say X-ray vision
can't penetrate lead,

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:18.080
and it's true. Lead has 82 electrons,

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:21.360
which makes it a very
effective scatterer of X-rays.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:23.680
But gold has 79 electrons.

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:25.520
It should be just as good a scatterer.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:30.520
So, the images that we see
here for Clark's X-ray vision,

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:33.240
that, I'm giving a good grade to,

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:35.600
but it gets downgraded by being able

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:39.120
to actually create photons
of light from the eye

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:42.120
that get reflected back
and detected again.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:44.280
So there I'm gonna have to downgrade him.

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:47.720
I'll give him maybe five out of 10.

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:50.600
[screaming]

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:54.960
You don't have to be crazy
to do that, but it helps.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:58.160
So, she has some superpower

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:00.120
that enables her to create

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:03.760
these very high-intensity sound waves.

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:06.360
The sound waves are the same type of

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:08.760
propulsive force in an explosion.

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:12.480
Say, dynamite or something exploding.

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.320
People get knocked down, windows break,

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:17.200
buildings can be shattered

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:19.680
because of the air molecules

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:23.200
traveling so fast,
slamming into an object,

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:25.640
and it's the air molecules,
and they're doing it.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:33.240
OK. That...[laughs]

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:35.880
That's gonna cost you some points.

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:39.240
Here she's hanging onto the motorcycle,

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:41.240
maintaining her balance.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:44.000
I don't doubt that there
are skilled athletes

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:45.760
that can actually do this.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:49.240
And then as she catches
up with the other car

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:50.720
and is holding onto it

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:52.680
and then moves relative to it,

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:54.200
well, if she's holding onto the car,

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:56.560
she's traveling at the
same speed as the car.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:59.200
So her relative velocity
to the car is zero.

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.800
Then even when she moves a
little bit forward at all,

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:03.440
that only requires her

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:06.040
a small additional
relative velocity forward

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:07.840
to get to the front of the car.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:11.640
Harley Quinn maintaining
perfect roller-skating form

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:15.480
during this explosion, not so good.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:17.720
So I give this maybe a six out of 10.

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:20.840
Shazam: Your phone's charged.

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:22.000
Your phone's charged.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:22.840
Freddy: Superpowers.

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:24.320
Shazam: And your phone's charged.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:25.160
Well, you think you can do better?

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:27.040
Jim: Yep, and the phone blows up,

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:28.640
because phones are not supposed

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:30.880
to be charged up by lightning!

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:33.520
The batteries work by a chemical reaction.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:36.920
Chemical A and chemical
B go to chemical C and D,

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:38.240
and they do it in such a way that

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:40.880
they build up electrical
charges on the terminals,

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:43.320
and then you can use
those electrical charges

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:45.400
to power your phone.

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:47.440
And once you've used up

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:50.480
all of the chemical A and B,

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:53.760
you can force the
reaction to run backwards,

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:57.360
and go backwards from
C to D back to A and B.

00:20:57.360 --> 00:20:59.440
And that's recharging your battery.

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:01.720
Zapping it in this way

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:04.880
is not only not going
to recharge the battery,

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:07.400
but it's gonna fry

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:11.880
all of the microchips that
you have in the phone.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:15.200
He's a nice boy, but I give
this only a two out of 10.

